{title: The Lighthouse Keeper}
{artist: Campfire Tests}
{key: D}
{tempo: 96}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
The [D]keeper climbed the [G]stair at dusk with [D]oil and wick and [A]flame
He [D]trimmed the lamp and [G]wiped the glass and [A]wrote the ship's last [D]name
The [Bm]wind came off the [G]headland and the [D]rain came off the [A]sea
And [D]every light he [G]ever lit was [A]lit for you and [D]me
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
His [D]father kept the [G]tower and his [D]father's father [A]too
They [D]counted every [G]sail that passed and [A]logged them two by [D]two
The [Bm]ledger on the [G]table holds a [D]hundred years of [A]rain
And [D]every page is [G]signed in ink and [A]never signed in [D]vain
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus: Chorus}
So [G]turn the lamp and [D]keep it bright
[A]Somebody's sailing [D]home tonight
[G]Over the rocks and [D]under the moon
[A]Bring them in [D]soon
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
In [D]eighteen ninety [G]seven came a [D]storm out of the [A]west
It [D]tore the shutters [G]off the house and [A]left him little [D]rest
He [Bm]kept the beam a-[G]turning while the [D]tower shook and [A]swayed
And [D]nine men on a [G]schooner saw the [A]light and were not [D]afraid
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
The [D]winters on the [G]island were as [D]long as winters [A]go
He [D]mended nets and [G]painted rails and [A]shovelled out the [D]snow
His [Bm]daughter learned to [G]read the stars and [D]name them one by [A]one
And [D]wrote them on the [G]window frost be-[A]fore the rising [D]sun
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus: Chorus}
So [G]turn the lamp and [D]keep it bright
[A]Somebody's sailing [D]home tonight
[G]Over the rocks and [D]under the moon
[A]Bring them in [D]soon
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 5}
They [D]brought the wire in [G]nineteen ten and [D]brought the engine [A]too
The [D]lamp that needed [G]no one's hands was [A]bright and cold and [D]new
He [Bm]sat beside it [G]every night as [D]if it might for-[A]get
And [D]listened to the [G]foghorn and the [A]waves against the [D]jet
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 6}
His [D]daughter took the [G]ferry and she [D]never came back [A]home
She [D]wrote him from a [G]city made of [A]glass and brick and [D]stone
She [Bm]said the streets are [G]never dark, the [D]lamps are everywhere
But [D]none of them are [G]turning, Dad, and [A]no one's keeping [D]care
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 7}
The [D]tower stands there [G]empty now, the [D]keeper's long a-[A]sleep
The [D]light still turns a-[G]bove the rocks and [A]out across the [D]deep
And [Bm]if you sail that [G]water on a [D]wild and moonless [A]night
Just [D]know that someone [G]somewhere once was [A]keeping it a-[D]light
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus: Chorus}
So [G]turn the lamp and [D]keep it bright
[A]Somebody's sailing [D]home tonight
[G]Over the rocks and [D]under the moon
[A]Bring them in [D]soon
{end_of_chorus}
